WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:12.474
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:15.390
PETER DER MANUELIAN: It's always good to know who's doing the digging.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.900
So in this section, I will talk about George Andrew Reisner

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.090
short biographical sketch.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.390
Reisner was born in 1867 in Indianapolis.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.140
That makes him a Hooser.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.160
He came to Harvard in the class of 1889.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.170
And in the last few years of his studies,

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.590
he became interested in Semitic languages, ancient cultures

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.150
of the ancient Near East.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.820
He'd first heard about this in some of his high school classes

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.660
back in Indianapolis.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.750
He graduated in the class of 1889.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.830
And you see a class photograph here in this picture.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.610
Along the way, he studied under David Gordon Lyon.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.570
Lyon was the one who founded the Harvard Semitic Museum, got this building built

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.240
in 1903 that you see here, and he was really

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.540
the father of what is now the Department of Near Eastern Languages

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.500
and Civilizations.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.180
Reisner went home to Indianapolis after getting his BA degree

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.230
and worked in a law firm.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.590
And then he came back to Harvard for his master's degree in 1891,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.410
and his PhD in 1893.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.380
Along the way, he took time out to go back home and marry the girl

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.040
next door, Mary Bronson.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.290
David Gordon Lyon had studied in Leipzig,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.450
and he felt it was important for Reisner to have

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.390
the experience of a German academic institution,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.550
so he sent him off to Berlin.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.420
And it's there that Reisner began his Egyptological career.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.930
Remember at Harvard it was all about Semitic languages, so biblical, Hebrew,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.650
Akkadian, cuneiform inscriptions, and the like.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.050
It was at Berlin that he met the great Egyptologist,

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.620
Adolf Erman who had studied so many phases of the Egyptian language.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.930
And his star pupil Kurt Sethe.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.470
And in the Berlin museum, Reisner learned Egyptian -- all phases

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.500
of the language -- from Sethe.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.020
He thought everything was said after spending three years at Berlin,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.970
and he came back to Harvard and taught for a year, but to his great surprise,

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.750
there was no money to keep him.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.860
And so all seemed lost until he got a letter from Berlin

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.320
from one of his colleagues Ludwig Borchardt,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.190
inviting him to come to Cairo and be part of an international commission

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.400
to study and publish objects in the Egyptian Museum.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.270
This was his first good paying job, and of course, he jumped at it.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.850
So here is a photograph of the international commission

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.310
team members from several different countries

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.530
all working on different aspects of the Egyptian Museum collection.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.380
It was here in Cairo that Reisner met Phoebe Apperson

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.100
Hearst, mother of the newspaper magnate, William Randolph Hearst.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.710
She was looking for excavators to support projects

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:45.420
all over the world and Egypt too, for the University of California Berkeley.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.200
So in 1899, Hearst and Reisner signed an agreement,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.880
and he decided to change directions completely from Semitic philology

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.270
to Egyptian object publishing and become an archaeologist.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.820
This was a whole right turn for him.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.700
And the agreement called for him basically to learn on the job.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.280
Not a bad understanding if you can get it.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.830
And so he started in Upper Egypt southern parts

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.610
of the country working at quote-unquote, "less important sites" in order

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.940
to hone his craft, inventing survey techniques,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.790
and record keeping for his excavations.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.760
This map shows you some of the early sites that he worked at.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.150
And there's a photograph of the camp at Naga Adair, which came

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.660
to be a very important site ultimately.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:31.620
But it was later on in 1903 that the Giza necropolis became available.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.600
There had been just too much illicit digging,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.960
and Reisner was not the only one complaining to the Egyptian antiquities

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:37.800
authorities --

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.280
then, of course, under French control under Gaston Maspero battle.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.130
Maspero allowed several different expeditions to come together

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.350
and amicably divide up the site.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.800
And this is what happened.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.880
So in this section, I'm just going to talk about Reisner's excavations.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.800
In another module, you've seen how the site

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.180
was divided between the German, later German Austrian, the Italian

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.280
and the American concessions.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.750
This plan shows the dates in red type how Reisner moved all

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.520
across the necropolis.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.480
He began in 1904 at the far Western edge of the site

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.320
and moved gradually eastwards towards the Great Pyramid.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.860
Along the way, he picked up a student from Harvard, Albert Morton Lythgoe,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.320
who had transferred from Brown to come to Harvard

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.400
and had been trained at the American school in Athens and elsewhere.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.720
Lythgoe would later leave to become the first curator

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.720
at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.420
Everything was going just fine until Phoebe Hearst ran

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.060
into financial trouble and she needed to pull out

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.950
from supporting the excavation.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.180
This was in 1904 and 1905.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.340
And this was a catastrophe.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:43.200
So in the summer of 1905, Lythgoe came back to Harvard first,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.820
and Reisner followed later.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.180
And the idea was to try to get the Museum of Fine Arts Boston and

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.290
or Harvard University interested in picking up the excavation.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.500
So this began what I call the intriguing summer of 1905.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.710
And this photograph shows you many of the players.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:02.580
We have Harvard's President Eliot looking after Harvard's interests.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.890
David Gordon Lyon, Reisner's old professor,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.900
trying to do right by everyone.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.280
Albert Morton Lythgoe, Reisner supporter, and assistant,

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.350
but really had his own agenda along the way as well.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.350
And then on the museum side, Gardner Martin Lane

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.859
was representing the interests of the museum.

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:20.150
So there was a lot of back and forth and eventually was

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.830
created by the Harvard University Boston Museum of Fine Arts Expedition.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.840
Here's a photograph of the old Copley Square

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.420
building for the Museum of Fine Arts in those days.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.660
In 1909, the building moved to Huntington Avenue, the site

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.370
that it still occupies today.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:40.790
This was a one year contract and was meant to expire in 1906.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.130
But it continued as a sort of gentlemen's agreement

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.620
between these institutions for the next 40 years,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.610
all the way until Reisner death in 1942.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.890
And so the objects started flowing in.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.110
Here is a photograph of the MFA galleries.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.170
And you'll see some of the Giza masterpieces set up in the room.

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:01.430
Crates were arriving, records, photographs, notes, publications.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:06.320
Reisner was writing articles in the MFA bulletin and elsewhere.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.480
And all of this took place from the headquarters

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.820
at what was known as Harvard camp.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:12.560
Here you see it on this aerial photograph

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.220
the red arrow points to the location to the west of Khafre's pyramid.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.820
This was just a simple collection of mud brick huts,

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.590
but it eventually grew over the years.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:27.530
It had the engineers offices, the photo studio, Reisner's office, dining area,

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.154
and of course, sleeping quarters.

00:06:29.154 --> 00:06:31.070
This photograph shows you the courtyard, which

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:35.360
was always choked with objects, fragments of pottery, stone vessels.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.060
And from here, a lot of the colossal statues came together.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.560
There's also a computer model we've made of this area.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.720
And it's important to remember that Reisner was not

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.390
only famous for developing archeological method

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:51.020
and these great documentation systems at the Giza a pyramids,

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.630
but all up and down the Nile.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.240
Here is a photograph of him working all the way down south in Nubia.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.470
He was basically the father of Nubian archeology as well.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.620
This image shows one of the class notebooks.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.060
The class of 1889.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.160
And you can see how Reisner is aging.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.010
Eventually, by the 1930s he was the great eminence,

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.940
he was the sage up at Harvard camp, and everyone would come to consult him,

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.110
not only about matters archeological but political issues as well.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.360
Remember, you have the British running the government,

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.840
you have the French controlling the antiquities service, and the Egyptians,

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.420
of course desiring their own independence along the way.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.250
So this photograph from an Arabic newspaper shows him in advanced age.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.070
Unfortunately, he's losing his sight by this time.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:38.570
But again, he was the master, the expert whom so many came to consult.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.820
And I couldn't help noticing recently on the internet

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.340
this photograph of some mirrored sunglasses that

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.170
show the pyramids reflecting.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:49.490
And I got to chuckle because Reisner had done this already before.

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.760
We can compare this internet image to the photograph taken

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.850
from that Arabic newspaper, and you can see the pyramid

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.210
reflecting in Reisner glasses as well.

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:03.890
There was even a live broadcast the first time ever made by NBC

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:08.240
right from the pyramids on a cold winter evening in February 1938.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.780
Reisner and the team assembled on the north face of the Khufu's pyramid,

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:13.052
and they broadcast live.

00:08:13.052 --> 00:08:15.260
This would have been dinner time back home in the US,

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.050
but it was about 11:00 PM in Cairo.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:22.970
And he narrated some of the excavation workloads, and some of his assistants

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.050
spoke as well.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:27.710
And a famous radio personality inside the King's Chamber inside Khufu

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:28.880
also broadcast.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.419
This required miles of cable going down to the Mena House Hotel

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.580
off the edge of the plateau.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.440
And then briefly just some of his trusted assistance.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.520
There was Dows Dunham, a Harvard student who had come out to Giza

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.590
and became Reiser's right-hand man.

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:45.350
Also working in Nubia and then eventually coming back

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:47.780
to the Museum of Fine Arts being, in all but name,

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:51.950
the chief curator as all the crates and the objects in the monuments came in.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.010
There was another Harvard student named William Stevenson Smith.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:58.880
He too eventually became a successor and curator in the Egyptian department

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:00.290
after Reisner's death.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.870
Great art historian who published many important

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:07.100
works about the Old Kingdom and the pyramid age.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.920
And then Reisner befriended a couple Joseph Lindon Smith

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.480
and his wife, Corinna.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:15.110
Smith was a painter who had studied at the School of the Museum of Fine

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:16.250
Arts and elsewhere.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.430
Turning from portrait painting, he found his true love coming out

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:25.490
to Egypt every year and painting scenes in Giza tombs, temples, Nubia, Egypt,

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:26.360
everywhere.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:30.200
So he became great friends with Reisner, and they enjoyed their company

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.200
very much.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.180
Reisner was not such a great fan of the burgeoning color photography process,

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:39.480
but he thought the oil paintings made by Smith were tremendously valuable.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.380
And there are well over a hundred of them today.

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:46.250
And then finally we have Said Ahmed Said, one of his kuftis.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.620
These are the Egyptian foreman from a town

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.430
in Upper Egypt who ran the show and Said Ahmed

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.310
was one of Reisner's most trusted individuals.

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.980
Took him on as a photographic helper and a boy,

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.320
and he spent decades with the expedition.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.000
Unfortunately, he died in 1926 during the excavation of the Hetepheres tomb.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.740
But Said's sons, Mohammed and Mahmoud went on to follow in this tradition,

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.740
and they ran everything at the expedition after that.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:17.346
Keeping the accounts, keeping the men in line, doing the pay stubs for everyone.

00:10:17.346 --> 00:10:19.220
One of them learned English and was typing up

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:22.190
a lot of the manuscripts as well.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:25.550
Reisner was only able to come back to America four or five times

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:26.570
to teach at Harvard.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:29.600
Remember World War I got in the way and delayed things.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.280
And his very last trip was in 1939 when he got an honorary degree

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.540
at the Harvard commencement.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.930
This was his 50th commencement anniversary.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:41.480
After that, he returned to his beloved Harvard camp.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.910
Remember he's pretty much blind by this time.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:45.260
World War II breaks out.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.360
He sends his wife and daughter home to America.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.200
They had quite a circuitous route, but they finally made it.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:55.670
And in 1942, unfortunately, after a long stroke and basically being bedridden,

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:00.020
Reisner died at Harvard camp behind the pyramids.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:03.440
He's buried in the old part of Cairo in the American cemetery.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.280
And his men and the Museum of Fine Arts team composed this text

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.812
that you see on the screen here.

00:11:08.812 --> 00:11:10.520
And this is the inscription that you read

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.430
when you visit his tombstone today.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.390
Back at Giza, you can still see vestiges of the great era of excavations:

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:20.900
some of these rusting debris carts and even railroad tracks --

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.980
Some of them still used today -- that were used to move the large mounds off

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:30.040
of the mastaba tombs during all those decades of excavation.

